00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
quality of life is not just about how  much money you make or how much money  you have to spend on parks when quality  of life is how people feel about their  community hi I'm Alexis Garcia with  Reason TV here at the Atlantic City web  conference in Los Angeles and right now  I'm joined by Dan walling he is the  mayor of Flint Michigan so mayor Hualien  you know we've kind of seen the plight  that's befallen Detroit can you talk a  little bit about what's happened in your  town yeah Flint to Detroit very similar  dynamics we're going through a  generational change the restructuring of  the global automotive industry the  change in family's preferences for  housing and shopping and how our  communities that boomed in the earlier  part of the 20th century aren't well  designed for the 21st century economy  for expectations of the 21st century  neighborhood or shopping and commercial  districts so we're having to look at our  entire community all 33 square miles and  figure out how every one of those places  can be vibrant and valuable in the 21st  century I found it interesting because I  heard you speak before and the statistic  about almost a little over 30% of the  space in your town is brownfields or you  know unused commercial Lots or abandoned  homes what are you guys kind of looking  at to put that to use yeah it's it's a  huge challenge and it's vacant  commercial property vacant residential  property old school buildings they're no  longer used that are now closed so we we  have this vacant property all across the  city  but when we were at the peak the city of  Flint was really overstuffed I mean  there were people living on top of  people for all the jobs available at the  General Motors factories so it's not  necessarily a bad thing when we look at  our long term prospects to have some  room in the city where we can expand our  green space along the Flint River we can  create larger educational campuses even  in our neighborhoods to be able to offer  more than a small house on a 40-foot lot  to be able to offer a developer an  opportunity to build a larger home on a  larger lot so  we're having to rethink the patterns of  our neighborhoods and our business  districts and our college and university  campuses but this vacant property does  give us a real opportunity to design our  city for the 21st century  now speaking of design you and some  other city leaders have put together  kind of a plan I think it's the imagined  Flint plain kind of looking forward and  when you were going through that you  kind of realized that some of these  zoning codes don't really fit that plan  can you talk a little bit about the  reform that you're looking at there yeah  we have a new master plan that's adapted  in we're revising our zoning code  because all of this was really designed  for the 1950s and the 1960s the big  production economy the single use  neighborhood the separated commercial  shopping districts that were all auto  oriented well here today in the 21st  century those dynamics have changed we  want mixed-use environments where you  can live work learn play you can walk  you can get on a bus so that means our  roads need to go on diets make room for  bikes and for buses and pedestrians it  means that our zoning needs to allow a  small let's say manufacturer not a not a  dirty old industrial facility but a new  manufacturer that's designing clothes or  a small electrical part in a supply  chain that can be done in a commercial  district with loft living upstairs in  the building next to the college or the  university so those are the kind of  innovative places that we're trying to  create in our new zoning code we're  thinking about place making not  separating uses but bringing uses  together and doing that in a very  extreme sense of the word where we're  not only having housing and shopping  which is normally what you think of with  mixed-use we think of extreme mixed use  of housing shopping learning playing in  parks and also the productive aspect of  of manufacturing which is something our  city's been known for for a long time  this is now just a new generation of  manufacturing we had carriages we had  cars now we're involved in diverse 21st  century advanced manufacturing with  medical devices and electrical  components and supplies for the auto  industry and transportation and  distribution companies that are beyond  just automobiles were part all that in  Flint that's sort of diversifying right  and do you see that you know the City  Council and the residents they're kind  of open to kind of tackling some of  these outdated laws yeah we have our  differences but the amazing thing about  this two-year process we went through to  envision a new future was it ended with  a plan that was able to be adopted  unanimously by our Planning Commission  and our City Council and that really  told me we have districts for our  council and our planning tells me that  people all across the city really saw  the benefit of this new vision and we're  using it to attract new investments and  to better use our existing federal  resources and also to get volunteers  involved because people want to be a  part of making these positive changes  they didn't know where to start  this framework now shows citizens and  community groups and black clubs how  they can be involved in moving the  community forward as part of a bigger  effort when you take office do you kind  of went to I think there's nine  different one yeah and you know you  really engaged the neighborhoods I think  you've seen in kind of our series  looking out Detroit where some of these  you know private citizens and private  groups kind of stepped in what have you  seen in Flint you know citizens stepping  in to deal with some of these things  like neighborhood blight there's some  incredible successes and it's rethinking  City Hall just like we've had to rethink  our economy and rethink our  neighborhoods the idea that government  is going to provide all of these  programs government has to be a partner  and has to facilitate the planning and  and the organizational infrastructure  but then it's it's citizen groups its  churches its schools and businesses that  are really the ones who are refining the  plans making sure what we're doing is  appropriate in each area and then  providing the the real people power to  actually get the work done to adopt the  park and turn it into a Youth Center  with a partnership with the Boys and  Girls Club in the old days in Flint that  would have been a city of Flint Parks  and Rec program now it's the city of  Flint with a Memorandum of Understanding  with  KREM Fitness Foundation and the Boys &amp;  Girls Club and a series of neighborhood  groups working through keep Genesee  County beautiful to actually get out  every couple weeks and and do the work  in the park to make it the kind of  facility that we want for our families I  think you see like a lot of these cities  are you know filling the squeeze  fiscally do you see that as a model  going forward that you know the the city  structure is gonna be kind of where some  of the organization takes place but it's  really gonna be a partnership with  community groups yeah I think it has to  be and and when we went out and tested  that and talked about it with our  community it wasn't just the sense that  well government has less money so we're  gonna have to rely on citizens community  groups and citizens we're really saying  no as a as a first option we want this  to be a partnership now if you go and  get some more money then that's a good  thing for all of us we want that  government resource but we want to be in  the driver's seat we want to be a part  of this plan and implementing it in our  neighborhood in our business corridor so  even as we recover economically and  build our tax base back our citizens  really demanded that they're at the top  of every one of our strategies dealing  with blight dealing with Public Safety  being more involved in education it's  got to be citizens and families first  yeah because they live there so they  would have a better idea of what needs  to be done then you know some bureaucrat  in DC right that's right and that's what  creates the real quality of life you  know quality of life is not just about  how much money you make or how much  money you have to spend on parks or in  quality of life is how people feel about  their community and the more they're  involved in shaping the plans and then  doing the work and seeing others you  know support them then that that's a  completely better and higher quality of  life than maybe a program that looks  really pretty and has a lot of money but  has never really connected with the  citizens so we've been forced to learn  those hard lessons in Flint but I think  it's a good foundation for us to move  forward from for mayor well and thank  you so much for joining us today  you're welcome Thanks and for reason TV  I'm Alexis Garcia

